WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.291
One of the most memorable things about a movie
character is how they're introduced to the

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.240
viewer, and how the film lets the viewer know
who they are and why they matter.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.280
With that in mind, here are some of the best
character introductions in the history of

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:12.780
film.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.500
​Vito Corleone

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:21.120
Francis Ford Coppola's 1972 masterpiece The
Godfather opens with Amerigo Bonasera telling

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:25.500
a complicated story that leads to an account
of his daughter's brutal assault at the hands

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:26.220
of two men.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:30.620
It's clear he's talking to someone highly
respected and very dangerous, and as the shot

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:34.200
grows wider, we’re introduced to this mysterious
man from behind.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.720
When Bonasera gets up to whisper in Don Corleone's
ear, we understand exactly what kind of justice

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:40.220
he seeks.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.520
“That I cannot do.”

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:48.350
Corleone doesn't betray any emotion in response
to what he's been asked, but takes Bonasera

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:49.560
to task nonetheless.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:54.760
We meet him as the Godfather, a man so powerful
he literally decides who lives and who dies.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:56.680
Hannibal Lecter

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.440
When FBI trainee Clarice Starling enters the
dungeon-like hallway that leads to Hannibal

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.470
Lecter's cell, viewers have already heard
about the brutal, cannibalistic serial killer

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:06.580
she's on her way to meet.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.520
Starling sees a succession of horrifying men
in the cells along the way.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.860
So, after all this setup, it's something of
a surprise when Lecter finally comes into

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:15.360
view.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.100
His cell is clean and has art hanging on the
walls.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.800
“Doctor, my name is Clarice Starling.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:21.680
May I speak with you?"

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:24.440
"You're one of Jack Crawford's, aren't you?"

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:26.480
"I am, Yes.”

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.750
Even as he's being friendly to Clarice, there's
something unnerving about him.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.780
He's being polite, but he's also testing her
in every moment.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:38.540
It's not that Hannibal Lecter isn't a monster,
it's just that he's an extraordinarily civilized

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:39.040
one.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:40.640
Batman

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:45.960
Tim Burton's 1989 version of Batman begins
with a scene that suggests the character's

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:50.090
well-known origin, as a boy and his parents
are accosted by hoodlums in the back alleys

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.040
of Gotham City.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:54.100
These aren't the Waynes, however, and they
survive the mugging.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.320
The two criminals find a nearby perch and
start sorting through their spoils, while

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.780
discussing recent sightings of a batlike figure
attacking criminals.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.920
As they talk, the recognizable silhouette
of Batman descends behind them before the

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.860
Dark Knight easily kicks their butts.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:09.440
“Don't kill me man!"

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.719
"I'm not going to kill you, I want you to
do me a favor.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.120
I want you to tell all your friends about
me."

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:15.060
"WHAT ARE YOU?"

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:17.700
"I’m Batman.”

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:19.700
Darth Vader

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.380
A brigade of white-armored stormtroopers breach
the rebel spaceship Tantive IV first, engaging

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:29.020
in a shootout that leaves many rebels dead
and the ship filled with smoke.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.520
With the rebels on the run, the stormtroopers
stand at attention as Darth Vader enters the

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:34.020
scene.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:38.680
It's immediately clear: Vader is extremely
powerful, and he doesn't do the grunt work.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.169
The nature of Darth Vader's power will become
clear in later scenes, but for now it's enough

00:02:43.169 --> 00:02:45.800
that he's very big, very scary, and in charge.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:47.420
Quint

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:52.640
Steven Spielberg's 1975 classic Jaws is a
movie about a deadly shark.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:57.219
But it's also a movie about a town in crisis,
selfishly searching for a solution to keep

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:58.720
their tourism industry alive.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.100
This comes to a head in a scene where all
the town leaders are meeting and everyone

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:03.120
is talking at once.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:16.420
Robert Shaw’s Quint enters Jaws very late,
but this scene quickly makes clear what sort

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:17.380
of man he is.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:21.720
He's extremely competent, and he knows it,
but he's also disinterested in polite social

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:22.220
norms.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:26.780
He's not the guy anyone wants to work with,
but he's the guy who can catch a giant shark.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.920
"$10,000 for me by myself.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.540
For that you get the head, the tail, the whole
damn thing."

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:36.820
Thanks for watching!

00:03:37.189 --> 00:03:39.700
